# Task completion

$ARGUMENTS — the task branch. If not given: `git branch --show-current`.

Full protocol and the completion invariant:
[docs/git-workflow.md](../../../docs/git-workflow.md).

> **This skill is the final gate.** Do not run the project-wide checks by hand
> right before invoking it — that pays twice for the same work. While coding,
> quick local checks are enough; the full pass happens once, here.

> **Run the gate SYNCHRONOUSLY** — an ordinary Bash call with a generous
> timeout, never in the background. Backgrounded output is buffered, looks
> empty, and invites minutes of polling followed by a second run of the same
> command. Write output to a file and filter that file with grep; never re-run
> the gate just to see it filtered differently.

> **"Merge and push after every commit" means steps 1–5 already ran during the
> task.** Here they close out the last commit.

## Step 1 — Gate

{{.GateInstructions}}

A red gate means the task is not finished. Fix it; do not suppress it, do not
skip tests to get it green.

## Step 2 — Sync the docs

1. **`{{.QueueFile}}`** — delete the finished task's pointer line, and only that
   line. A pointer is a LINE NUMBER: verify against the file itself
   (`sed -n 'NNp' <file>`), never from memory; other edits move it.
2. If the task's own file or table tracks status, mark it done with the date.
3. If this project keeps capability/architecture docs, a decision log or a bug
   tracker, update whatever entries this task affects.
4. If you inserted or deleted lines in a file that pointers address, re-index
   every affected pointer and verify each with `sed -n 'NNp'`.

## Step 3 — Commit the docs

If the doc updates were not part of the code commit, commit them separately.

## Step 4 — Merge into {{.MainBranch}}

Committing on `{{.MainBranch}}` directly is forbidden — the status edit travels
in the branch and arrives as part of the merge.

```bash
git -C <repo-root> merge --no-ff $ARGUMENTS -m "Merge $ARGUMENTS: <one line>"
```

If the root checkout holds someone else's uncommitted files and blocks the
merge, do it in a throwaway worktree from the remote:

```bash
git worktree add .claude/worktrees/merge-tmp -b merge-tmp-$ARGUMENTS origin/{{.MainBranch}}
git -C .claude/worktrees/merge-tmp status --short        # MUST be empty
git -C .claude/worktrees/merge-tmp merge --no-ff $ARGUMENTS -m "Merge $ARGUMENTS: <one line>"
git -C .claude/worktrees/merge-tmp push origin HEAD:{{.MainBranch}}
git worktree remove .claude/worktrees/merge-tmp && git branch -D merge-tmp-$ARGUMENTS
```

`--no-ff` is required.

## Step 5 — Push

```bash
git push origin {{.MainBranch}}
```

## Step 6 — Free the slot, then delete the branch

Order matters: while the slot holds the branch, `git branch -d` refuses.

```bash
bash scripts/worktree-pool.sh release p1-work
git branch -d $ARGUMENTS
git push origin --delete $ARGUMENTS     # if the branch was published
```

The slot itself is **not** removed — it is persistent.

## Step 7 — Verify

```bash
git log --oneline --graph -5
git status --short
bash scripts/worktree-pool.sh list
```

The merge commit is visible, the tree is clean, the slot is on a detached HEAD.
Report what was merged, the gate result, and which pointer left the queue. Then
stop — do not start the next task in this session.
